# Versioning

The manager's version is in three places that must match: `VERSION`, `package.json` `version` and
`package-lock.json` `version` (a unit test checks this). Running managers compare `VERSION` with GitHub's
`main` branch and offer an update when it is higher, so a bump reaches every user.

## Rules

- **Never bump the version on your own**, not even after a big change. Only change it when the user gives the
  new number in their request (for example "release 1.2.0" or "bump to 1.1.1").
- If a change seems to deserve a release, say so at the end and suggest a number; do not apply it.
- Versions only go up. Never lower a published version (clients would stop seeing updates).
- Pull requests from contributors do not touch the version.

## Choosing the number (when asked to suggest one)

`MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`, with minor bumps as the normal case:

- **Patch** (1.2.0 -> 1.2.1): bug fixes, text, styling, docs.
- **Minor** (1.2.1 -> 1.3.0): new features or sections, new device support, behaviour changes users can keep up
  with. Most releases.
- **Major** (1.3.0 -> 2.0.0): reserved for big changes: a new workspace or settings layout that needs migration,
  a higher Node.js requirement, removed features, or a redesign.

## Applying a version the user gave

```bash
npm version 1.3.0 --no-git-tag-version   # package.json + package-lock.json
printf '1.3.0\n' > VERSION
node --test test/unit.test.ts --test-name-pattern "VERSION"
```

Then commit as `Version 1.3.0` (see the `manager-release` skill).
